Right, setting the stage for success.

119
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:37,140
Exactly.

120
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:41,390
Now that everyone feels comfortable, it's time to get clear on the why and the what.

121
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:42,830
This isn't just admin.

122
00:05:43,030 --> 00:05:46,360
It's about removing any confusion, setting that roadmap.

123
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:51,240
Yeah, the sources point to three really essential steps here, foundational steps.

124
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:52,740
And why are these so important?

125
00:05:52,900 --> 00:05:59,010
Well, if you zoom out, clear communication right at the start is just, it's the bedrock of good collaboration, isn't it?

126
00:05:59,030 --> 00:05:59,150
Sure.

127
00:05:59,650 --> 00:06:04,260
These three steps give everyone that mental map, how the meeting will run, what they'll get out of it.

128
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:10,520
It stops confusion before it starts, manages expectations, gets everyone aligned on the purpose before you dive deep.

129
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:14,350
Okay, so step one, clearly state the goal or the purpose.

130
00:06:14,750 --> 00:06:16,030
Laser focus needed here.

131
00:06:16,210 --> 00:06:16,910
Yep.

132
00:06:16,930 --> 00:06:22,460
You might have a really defined objective like, our goal today is to plan the social media strategy for March and for April.

133
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:23,630
Very concrete.

134
00:06:23,790 --> 00:06:25,350
Or you could frame it by the outcome.

135
00:06:25,570 --> 00:06:29,740
Like, by the end of this session, everyone will know how to use the new accounting software.

136
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:30,580
Exactly.

137
00:06:30,880 --> 00:06:31,800
Outcome focused.

138
00:06:31,780 --> 00:06:35,250
And it doesn't have to be super formal either, even for a casual chat.

139
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:36,650
Like the book club example.

140
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:37,070
Right.

141
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:40,640
You could just say, we're here today to talk about George Orwell's novel 1984.

142
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:42,600
Or was it 1986?

143
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:47,470
Anyway, that clarity helps people focus their thoughts.

144
00:06:47,790 --> 00:06:48,550
Okay, got it.

145
00:06:48,570 --> 00:06:53,860
So purpose first, then step two is outlining the agenda or the structure.

146
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:59,010
If you sent out an agenda beforehand, now's the time to refer to it.

147
00:06:59,050 --> 00:06:59,920
How would you phrase that?

148
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:02,860
Something simple like, I'll just draw your attention to the agenda.

149
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:04,320
We've got a few things to get through today.

150
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:07,220
And then, naturally, you'd quickly mention the main items.

151
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:08,360
Okay.

152
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:10,220
But what if there isn't a formal agenda?

153
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:14,870
Happens all the time, especially for more informal or spontaneous meetings.

154
00:07:14,930 --> 00:07:17,810
Then you, as the host, need to verbally outline the structure.

155
00:07:18,350 --> 00:07:20,570
This is where signposting language is your best friend.

156
00:07:20,910 --> 00:07:23,640
You mean using words like first, second, then?

157
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:24,360
Exactly.

158
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:27,560
First, second, then, after that, next, and finally.

159
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:31,990
They act like verbal breadcrumbs guiding everyone along the path.

160
00:07:32,170 --> 00:07:32,730
Makes sense.

161
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:35,050
It manages expectations about what's coming.

162
