164
00:07:36,510 --> 00:07:40,140
You might say, okay, so the first item on the agenda is social media.

165
00:07:40,520 --> 00:07:44,140
And then we'll move on to item number two, which is customer satisfaction.

166
00:07:45,380 --> 00:07:47,230
After that, we'll take a look at customer feedback.

167
00:07:47,650 --> 00:07:49,890
And finally, we'll discuss the new marketing strategy.

168
00:07:50,490 --> 00:07:51,150
Crystal clear.

169
00:07:51,610 --> 00:07:52,630
Everyone knows the flow.

170
00:07:53,630 --> 00:07:55,400
Okay, so purpose, agenda.

171
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,440
What's the third essential step?

172
00:07:58,860 --> 00:07:59,460
Ground rules.

173
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:03,840
Or housekeeping, as it's often called.

174
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:04,640
Housekeeping.

175
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:05,730
Sounds like tidying up.

176
00:08:05,850 --> 00:08:06,290
Huh.

177
00:08:06,310 --> 00:08:06,690
Yeah.

178
00:08:06,710 --> 00:08:09,490
But in a meeting context, it means the sort of administrative bits.

179
00:08:09,930 --> 00:08:12,550
The rules of engagement that help things run smoothly.

180
00:08:13,010 --> 00:08:16,320
It's about proactively managing potential disruptions.

181
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:17,200
Ah, right.

182
00:08:17,520 --> 00:08:18,820
Ensuring smooth sailing.

183
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:20,040
How do you introduce that?

184
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:24,050
A nice transition is before we dive in, we just have to get through some housekeeping first.

185
00:08:24,060 --> 00:08:25,090
Sets the expectation.

186
00:08:25,070 --> 00:08:27,050
And what kind of things fall under housekeeping?

187
00:08:27,130 --> 00:08:29,830
Common ones are managing distractions, like phones.

188
00:08:30,290 --> 00:08:36,180
You might say, if you have your phone with you, please take a moment just to turn it on silent or to switch it off just so we don't have any disruptions.

189
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:37,340
Gentle but clear.

190
00:08:37,480 --> 00:08:37,880
Good one.

191
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:38,660
What else?

192
00:08:38,780 --> 00:08:42,910
Maybe setting a protocol for questions, especially if you need to stick to time.

193
00:08:43,370 --> 00:08:46,520
If you have any questions outside of the agenda, just save them for the end.

194
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:48,240
Helps keep things focused.

195
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:49,200
Makes sense.

196
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:51,740
And for online meetings, this stuff feels even more important.

197
00:08:51,880 --> 00:08:53,390
It can get chaotic otherwise.

198
00:08:53,490 --> 00:08:55,010
Oh, absolutely vital online.

199
00:08:55,310 --> 00:08:56,510
You need clear instructions.

200
00:08:56,850 --> 00:09:04,360
Things like, if you have any questions during our discussion today, please just use the raised hand reaction or raise your hand so I can see it on screen.

201
00:09:04,380 --> 00:09:04,620
Right.

202
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:06,400
Using the platform's tools.

203
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:07,400
Exactly.

204
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:07,700
Yeah.

205
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:11,680
And maybe feel free to share your comments and your questions in the chat.

206
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:16,490
And definitely please make sure you put your microphones on mute to keep disruptions to a minimum.

207
00:09:16,650 --> 00:09:20,100
Those simple instructions are total game changers online, aren't they?

208
00:09:20,660 --> 00:09:22,940
Makes participation much less awkward.

209
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:23,490
Totally.

210
00:09:23,750 --> 00:09:24,910
Everyone knows how to engage.

211
00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:27,810
Okay, so the meeting's launched.

212
00:09:27,830 --> 00:09:28,720
Everyone's welcomed.

213
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:31,180
They know the purpose, the agenda, the rules.

214
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:33,660
Now the real work begins, right?

215
00:09:34,350 --> 00:09:35,710
Managing the actual discussion.

216
00:09:35,730 --> 00:09:37,490
This is where the host really steps up, yeah.

217
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:38,490
Guiding the flow.

218
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:40,460
And it's a tricky balance, isn't it?

219
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:47,150
Keeping things effective, productive, on schedule, but without shutting people down or stifling good ideas.

220
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:49,890
It's a real art, and it raises that key question.

221
00:09:50,470 --> 00:10:00,050
How do you make sure everyone feels heard but still keep the meeting moving towards its goal, especially when, let's be honest, conversations can easily wander off track?

222
00:10:00,070 --> 00:10:00,790
Definitely.

223
00:10:00,810 --> 00:10:11,890
So a skilled facilitator, a good host, uses specific language for a few key things, prompting discussion, clarifying points, and crucially, managing time and keeping things focused.

224
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Okay, let's break those down.

225
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:16,130
First up, prompting discussion, getting people talking.

226
00:10:16,510 --> 00:10:18,410
Sometimes you need to give things a little nudge.

227
00:10:18,670 --> 00:10:19,010
You do.

228
00:10:19,470 --> 00:10:22,120
You might kick things off with something collaborative.

229
00:10:22,140 --> 00:10:24,840
To kick things off, why don't we brainstorm a few ideas together?

230
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:25,240
Yeah.

231
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:29,080
It's generally good advice to start broad, ask general questions first.

232
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:30,650
Before singling anyone out.

233
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:30,870
Right.

234
00:10:30,990 --> 00:10:36,050
To encourage more organic input initially, so general opinion-seeking phrases work well.

235
00:10:36,190 --> 00:10:36,550
Like what?

236
00:10:36,650 --> 00:10:38,460
Things like, what does everyone else think?

237
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:41,740
Or, what are your thoughts on the survey results?

238
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:44,830
Maybe, I'd like to get your input on the new design.

239
00:10:45,690 --> 00:10:46,890
They're open invitations.

240
00:10:47,410 --> 00:10:49,650
But what if you ask, what does everyone think?

241
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,680
And you just get silence, crickets.

242
00:10:55,020 --> 00:10:57,090
The dreaded silence.

243
00:10:57,110 --> 00:11:00,910
Yeah, that's when you might need to gently call on someone specific.

244
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:02,330
Still encouragingly, though.

245
00:11:02,710 --> 00:11:04,450
Like, what do you think about that, Amy?

246
00:11:04,570 --> 00:11:05,530
Okay, a gentle nudge.

247
00:11:06,240 --> 00:11:07,500
Now, what about clarification?

248
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:12,110
Someone says something and you need a bit more detail or you're not quite sure you grasped it.

249
00:11:12,290 --> 00:11:16,920
Yeah, you need to understand fully but without making them feel like they were unclear or that you weren't listening.

250
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:40,600
exactly how do you ask for that well you can be direct can you explain that in a bit more detail or maybe would you mind going over that again please and going over something means more than just repeating right exactly it implies explaining it again maybe more carefully adding detail ensuring nothing critical is missed okay and there's another technique mentioned paraphrasing that seems really useful

251
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:44,030
Oh, it's a fantastic conversation skill, especially in meetings.

252
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:46,090
Paraphrasing for confirmation.

253
00:11:46,130 --> 00:11:47,170
How does that work?

254
00:11:47,190 --> 00:11:52,360
You basically restate what you think they said in your own words and ask if you got it right.

255
00:11:53,400 --> 00:11:56,300
It checks understanding without making them repeat themselves.

256
00:11:56,640 --> 00:11:57,440
Could you give an example?

257
00:11:57,720 --> 00:11:57,920
Sure.

258
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:02,750
You could say, okay, so what you're saying is you're not really concerned about the deadline as long as the job's done well.

259
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:03,410
Is that correct?

260
00:12:03,970 --> 00:12:04,710
Ah, I see.

261
00:12:05,330 --> 00:12:10,540
Or maybe something like, if I understood correctly, you're saying that all of these figures are incorrect.

262
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:11,180
Precisely.

263
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:18,050
And sometimes you might need to simplify or rephrase what someone said for the benefit of the whole group.

264
00:12:18,070 --> 00:12:19,660
To make sure everyone's on the same page.

265
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:20,240
Exactly.

266
00:12:20,660 --> 00:12:22,880
So in other words, the report won't be finished on time.

267
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:27,090
It shows you're actively processing and ensuring shared understanding.

268
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:28,930
That's strong facilitation.

269
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:29,480
Right.

270
00:12:29,560 --> 00:12:33,270
OK, so beyond getting input and clarifying, there's the big one.

271
00:12:33,850 --> 00:12:36,470
Time management, keeping things on track.

272
00:12:36,910 --> 00:12:39,760
Nobody enjoys a meeting that just drags on forever.

273
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:42,000
No, definitely not.

274
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:45,200
And as the host, that's largely your responsibility.

275
00:12:45,620 --> 00:12:50,290
You need to manage the time effectively, but without being, you know, the meeting police.

276
00:12:50,430 --> 00:12:50,710
Yeah.

277
00:12:50,970 --> 00:12:52,450
How do you do that gracefully?

278
00:12:52,430 --> 00:13:01,230
Well, to move on from a point, especially if discussion is winding down, you can say, if nobody has anything else to add, let's move on.

279
00:13:01,290 --> 00:13:02,630
I'm conscious of time.

280
00:13:02,930 --> 00:13:04,210
Polite but firm.

281
00:13:04,730 --> 00:13:06,010
Signals the need to progress.

282
00:13:06,250 --> 00:13:06,700
Exactly.

283
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:08,120
It respects people's time.

284
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:10,540
But what if the discussion isn't winding down?

285
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:15,410
What if you genuinely need to cut someone off, maybe gently, to stick to the agenda?

286
00:13:15,430 --> 00:13:16,650
That feels awkward.

287
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:19,330
It can be, but sometimes necessary.

288
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:20,860
The key is how you do it.

289
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:24,220
You might need to say, I'm sorry, but I have to stop you there.

290
00:13:24,600 --> 00:13:26,060
Oof, that sounds harsh.

291
00:13:26,700 --> 00:13:27,040
It can.

292
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:29,780
So you immediately follow it with the reason, focusing on the shared goal.

293
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:31,350
We're running out of time, so we have to move on.

294
00:13:31,950 --> 00:13:32,830
It depersonalizes.

295
00:13:32,850 --> 00:13:34,430
It's about the agenda, not them.

296
00:13:34,710 --> 00:13:36,830
Okay, frame it around the shared constraint of time.

297
00:13:37,110 --> 00:13:39,950
And what if the conversation just goes completely off topic?

298
00:13:39,970 --> 00:13:43,520
Someone brings up something interesting but totally irrelevant to this meeting.

299
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,670
Yeah, the dreaded tangent.

300
00:13:45,690 --> 00:13:47,830
